  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nCurrently: light showers were noted on local radar along and west of \nthe Mississippi river. This activity was ahead of a band of showers \nand thunderstorms that extended from eastern Arkansas back across \nnorthwest Louisiana and east over northeast Texas. This band of \nstorms was nearly along a cold front emanating from a surface low \nover the central Plains. Latest satellite imagery/RAP showed the \ncirculation around a closed low spinning over northeast Kansas and \neastern Nebraska. This closed low will continue lifting to the \nnortheast while taking the surface low with it. Thus the cold front \nwill drag into the CWA later this afternoon with the band of storms \npreceding it. Nothing has changed in the previous thinking of the \npotential for locally heavy rainfall and isolated severe storms \nmainly over our northwest and the previous forecast discussion \nfollows. /22/\n\nTonight through Monday: A departing upper-level shortwave ridge is \nin the process of being replaced by a trough and deepening low \npressure system making its way into the central CONUS. The low \nitself is expected to lift northeastward today from the Southern \nHigh Plains to the Upper Midwest which should direct most forcing \nnorth and west of our forecast area, but a spoke of the shortwave \nenergy pivoting overhead during the afternoon and evening shows at \nleast modest height falls over the ArkLaMiss Delta region. Without \nprolonged warm, moist advection in advance of this system's cold \nfrontal passage, forecast instability is in the range of 500-1500 \nJ/kg over northeast LA during the daylight hours tomorrow but \ndecreasing to around 400-700 J/kg as the front moves further east \ninto Mississippi after dark. There is a modest 40kts of deep-layer \nshear expected across the front, but less favorable instability \nsuggests only low potential for pre-frontal convection to develop \ninto discreet supercells beyond northeast LA in the afternoon hours. \nThe primary hazards will be wind squalls with line segments into the \nearly morning hours and heavy rainfall rates. Weak tornadoes cannot \nbe ruled out. Heaviest rain rates could pose an isolated flash flood \nthreat in low-lying or urban areas, but the progressive movement of \nthe front should limit an organized flooding threat. The Marginal \nsevere storm risk remains in place for approximately the \nnorthwestern half of the area, and the \"Limited\" flash flood threat \ngraphic continues to highlight about the northwestern third. \n\nThis weather should clear by Wednesday, but showers could linger in \nthe wake of the front across eastern Mississippi Wednesday morning. \nThe pattern will reinforce mostly dry weather Wednesday into early \nnext week. Building high pressure over the weekend will push \ntemperatures below normal across our forecast area. High \ntemperatures Saturday through next Monday should be mostly in the \nmid to upper 60s or near 70 degrees, while overnight low from Friday \nnight onwards will dip into the 30s and 40s. Frost seems likely in \nthat time frame with any periods of particularly calm overnight \nwinds and mostly clear skies. Will continue to monitor trends, but \nwe are in the time of year where frost and light freeze are normal \nand the growing season has ended already.",
